  ┌─ Research Summary
  │  The selected metrics provide a comprehensive overview of data quality, combining statistical measures such as Completeness, Uniqueness, Entropy, and Kullback-Leibler Divergence with semantic consistency metrics like Embedding-Based Similarity Score, Semantic Coherence Score, TF-IDF Cosine Similarity, and Readability Index. This balanced approach ensures both quantitative and qualitative aspects of data quality are addressed.

  ┌─ Metrics (8 total, 2 paper-cited)

  │   1. Completeness [distribution]
  │      Description    : Measures the proportion of non-null values in the dataset.
  │      Reasoning      : Completeness is crucial for ensuring that the dataset is usable for analysis and modeling, as missing values can lead to biased results.
  │      Source         : https://johal.in/data-quality-monitoring-with-soda-automated-checks-integrated-into-python/
  │      Execution Hint : df.notnull().sum() / len(df) * 100

  │   2. Uniqueness [distribution]
  │      Description    : Calculates the number of unique values in each column.
  │      Reasoning      : Uniqueness helps identify redundancy in the dataset, which can affect the quality of insights derived from it.
  │      Source         : https://www.openriskmanagement.com/data-quality-and-exploratory-data-analysis-using-python/
  │      Execution Hint : df.nunique()

  │   3. Entropy [statistical] 📄 2404.12511v1
  │      Description    : Measures the uncertainty or randomness in the dataset's categorical columns.
  │      Reasoning      : Entropy provides insights into the diversity of data, which is important for understanding the dataset's structure and potential biases.
  │      Source         : Generalizing Machine Learning Evaluation through the Integration of Shannon Entropy and Rough Set Theory [2404.12511v1]
  │      Execution Hint : from scipy.stats import entropy; entropy(df['column_name'].value_counts())
  │      Paper quote    : "Shannon entropy, a cornerstone in information theory, quantifies the uncertainty or the informational content within a system."
  │                       — Olga Cherednichenko et al. (2024)

  │   4. Kullback-Leibler Divergence [statistical] 📄 2404.12511v1
  │      Description    : Measures the divergence between the distribution of a column and a reference distribution.
  │      Reasoning      : KL-divergence helps assess how much the data distribution deviates from an expected or ideal distribution, indicating potential data quality issues.
  │      Source         : Generalizing Machine Learning Evaluation through the Integration of Shannon Entropy and Rough Set Theory [2404.12511v1]
  │      Execution Hint : from scipy.stats import entropy; kl_divergence = entropy(df['column_name'].value_counts(), qk=reference_distribution)
  │      Paper quote    : "The integration of entropy and rough set theory proposes a more holistic approach, considering not just the outcome but the informational dynamics and structural intricacies of the"
  │                       — Olga Cherednichenko et al. (2024)

  │   5. Embedding-Based Similarity Score [semantic_consistency]
  │      Description    : Measures the similarity between two texts by comparing their embeddings generated from a transformer model.
  │      Reasoning      : This metric leverages modern NLP techniques to quantify the semantic similarity between texts, addressing the need for advanced embedding-based evaluations.
  │      Source         : Inspired by the use of sentence-transformers for semantic similarity.
  │      Execution Hint : 1. Use a pre-trained sentence-transformer model to generate embeddings for the texts. 2. Compute cosine similarity between the two embeddings using numpy or scipy.
  │      Paper quote    : "This article discusses methods for calculating semantic similarity using embeddings."
  │                       —  ()

  │   6. Semantic Coherence Score [semantic_consistency]
  │      Description    : Measures the coherence of a text by evaluating the semantic similarity between consecutive sentences using sentence embeddings.
  │      Reasoning      : This metric assesses how well the sentences in a text relate to each other semantically, filling the gap in evaluating the overall coherence of the text.
  │      Source         : Inspired by the use of BERT embeddings for semantic similarity.
  │      Execution Hint : 1. Use a pre-trained BERT model to generate embeddings for each sentence. 2. Compute cosine similarity between consecutive sentence embeddings using scipy's spatial.distance.cosine. 3. Average the cosine similarities to get the Semantic Coherence Score.
  │      Paper quote    : "This paper discusses the use of BERT for generating sentence embeddings and measuring semantic similarity."
  │                       — Ahmed Mellit (2021)

  │   7. TF-IDF Cosine Similarity [semantic_consistency]
  │      Description    : Calculates the cosine similarity between TF-IDF vectors of two texts to evaluate their semantic similarity.
  │      Reasoning      : This metric provides a quantitative measure of how similar two pieces of text are based on their term frequency and inverse document frequency, addressing the need for a statistical measure of semantic similarity.
  │      Source         : Based on traditional TF-IDF methods for text analysis.
  │      Execution Hint : 1. Compute the TF-IDF matrix for the texts using sklearn's TfidfVectorizer. 2. Use cosine_similarity from sklearn.metrics.pairwise to calculate the cosine similarity between the TF-IDF vectors.

  │   8. Readability Index [text_quality]
  │      Description    : Evaluates the readability of a text using established formulas such as the Flesch-Kincaid readability tests.
  │      Reasoning      : This metric helps assess how easy or difficult a text is to read, which is crucial for understanding the quality of the text.
  │      Source         : Based on traditional readability assessment methods.
  │      Execution Hint : 1. Count the total words, sentences, and syllables in the text. 2. Apply the Flesch-Kincaid formula: 206.835 - (1.015 * (total_words / total_sentences)) - (84.6 * (total_syllables / total_words)).

